<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuremberg trials</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as International Military Tribunal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials were international criminal trials held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> against leaders of defeated Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for invasions and atrocities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Between 20 November 1945 and 1 October 1946 the International Military Tribunal tried 22 of the most important surviving Nazi leaders and six German organisations; twelve defendants, among them Göring, Ribbentrop and Keitel, were sentenced to death. The United States held twelve further trials of lower-level perpetrators. Controversial for criminalising aggression retroactively, the trials are considered the beginning of international criminal law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials.jpg" alt="Nuremberg trials" width="330" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raymond D’Addario · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Defendants_in_the_dock_at_nuremberg_trials.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Between 1939 and 1945 Nazi Germany invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and war losses in the Soviet Union alone included 27 million dead, mostly civilians. In early 1942 nine governments-in-exile demanded an international court, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and United Kingdom declined, citing the failure of the prosecutions after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. At the London Conference, held from 26 June to 2 August 1945, the four powers negotiated the Nuremberg Charter, and until the end it was unclear that any trial would be held. It listed crimes against peace, crimes against humanity and war crimes. The American negotiator Robert H. Jackson threatened US withdrawal if aggression were not prosecuted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The tribunal opened on 20 November 1945. The defendants, who were largely unrepentant, included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>, the main target, Joachim von Ribbentrop, Wilhelm Keitel, Alfred Jodl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Albert Speer</a> and Rudolf Hess. Adolf Hitler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> had died by suicide. All pleaded not guilty. A charge of conspiracy, devised by the US War Department lawyer Murray C. Bernays, held together the array of charges and defendants. The prosecution entered 4,600 captured German documents into evidence.</p><p>Jackson gave the opening speech on 21 November. The Soviet prosecution produced the captured field marshal Friedrich Paulus as a witness. Defendants tried to blame Hitler, who was mentioned 12,000 times, and some claimed ignorance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. All eight closing statements on 31 August highlighted it. The judges called aggression the supreme international crime, though they did not define it or mention retroactivity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A planned second international trial of industrialists was never held because of differences among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> convened twelve further trials in the same courtroom, among them the Doctors' Trial, the Judges' Trial, the Ministries Trial and trials of industrialists in the Flick, IG Farben and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> cases. Of 2,500 identified major war criminals, 177 were tried. In a 1946 poll 78 per cent of Germans judged the trial fair, but four years later only 38 per cent did. Critics note that only the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> were prosecuted, and that trying acts not crimes at the time, above all crimes against peace, was controversial. The trials are regarded as the true beginning of international criminal law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuremberg trials” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Surviving Nazi officials were tried at the <b>Nuremberg trials</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The response to these crimes shaped the post-war order: the <b>Nuremberg trials</b> established that leaders could be judged for aggressive war, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and the Genocide Convention of 1948 and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Where dictatorships have ended, trials, truth commissions and memorials have recorded their crimes, from the <b>Nuremberg trials</b> of 1945–1946 to Argentina’s Trial of the Juntas in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
